## Adjusting the PoC

| What to change | Which file |
|----------------|-----------|
| Output fields to extract | `prompts/extraction_requirements.md` |
| Provider, model, or endpoint per stage | `config.yaml` → `stages` |
| Schema definition | `schemas/output_schema.py` |
| Evaluation metrics | `evals/run_basic_eval.py` |

Each `stages` entry is independent: `parser`, `extraction`, `transcription`,
`embedding`, `answer`, and `judge`. `provider_config.py` loads the selected provider's
credentials from `.env`. Set the model and optional base URL in that stage's entry.
Explicit stages take precedence over the legacy top-level provider/model settings.
Azure deployment names belong in `model`; LiteLLM models need a provider prefix.
Azure transcription deployment names belong in `transcription_model`. Set
`azure_endpoint` (or `azure_audio_endpoint` for a separate audio resource) when
overriding the Azure resource URL; an SDK `base_url` alone does not select an
audio resource.

Audio stages support OpenAI/Azure. For example, an Aitta extraction stage can use
`transcription: {provider: azure}` alongside
`extraction: {provider: aitta, model: google/gemma-4-31b-it}`. Vision needs a model
that accepts images. Embeddings need a model served by their own provider; native
Anthropic does not provide embeddings. Provider SDK extras are included in
`requirements.txt` for the selected blueprint stages; add the corresponding
`llm-google`, `llm-anthropic`, or `llm-litellm` extra if changing providers later.

Custom templates must call `get_stage_config(config, "stage")` when constructing
model components. The wizard rejects an older promoted template that lacks this
contract, preserving its pipeline for an explicit migration. A newly generated
generic skeleton still needs its labelled component calls wired before use.

